To apply a Gaussian filter before applying the Sobel operator, you can simply add a convolution step with a Gaussian kernel before the Sobel operator. Here's the modified code:

```matlab
% Read input image
input_image = imread('image.jpg');

% Convert to grayscale
gray_image = rgb2gray(input_image);

% Define Gaussian kernel
gaussian_kernel = fspecial('gaussian', [5 5], 0.5);

% Apply Gaussian filter
filtered_image = conv2(gray_image, gaussian_kernel, 'same');

% Define Sobel operator in x direction
sobel_x = [-1 0 1; -2 0 2; -1 0 1];

% Define Sobel operator in y direction
sobel_y = [-1 -2 -1; 0 0 0; 1 2 1];

% Apply Sobel operator in x direction
edge_image_x = conv2(filtered_image, sobel_x, 'same');

% Apply Sobel operator in y direction
edge_image_y = conv2(filtered_image, sobel_y, 'same');

% Combine edges in both directions
edge_image = sqrt(edge_image_x.^2 + edge_image_y.^2);

% Display edge image
imshow(edge_image);
```

In this modified code, we first define a Gaussian kernel using the `fspecial` function. We then apply the Gaussian filter to the grayscale image using the `conv2` function. Finally, we apply the Sobel operator as before, but now on the filtered image. This will result in a smoother edge image with reduced noise.

