To fix the CORS (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing) error in Next.js while using WordPress, you can follow these steps:

1. Install the `cors` package in your Next.js project. You can do this by running the following command in your terminal:
```bash
npm install cors
```
2. Create a new file named `cors.js` in your `pages` directory. This file will contain the CORS configuration for your application.
3. Open the `cors.js` file and add the following code:
```javascript
import { Cors } from 'cors';

const cors = new Cors({
  methods: ['GET', 'POST', 'PUT', 'DELETE'],
  origin: 'https://your-wordpress-site.com',
});

export default cors.handle;
```
Replace `https://your-wordpress-site.com` with the URL of your WordPress site.
4. In your `next.config.js` file, add the following code to configure the CORS middleware:
```javascript
module.exports = {
  // ...
  async headers() {
    return [
      {
        source: '/api/:path*',
        headers: [
          { key: 'Access-Control-Allow-Methods', value: 'GET, POST, PUT, DELETE' },
          { key: 'Access-Control-Allow-Headers', value: 'Content-Type' },
          { key: 'Access-Control-Allow-Credentials', value: 'true' },
          { key: 'Access-Control-Allow-Origin', value: 'https://your-wordpress-site.com' },
        ],
      },
    ];
  },
};
```
Replace `https://your-wordpress-site.com` with the URL of your WordPress site.
5. Save both files and restart your development server. Now, when you make requests to your WordPress site from your Next.js application, the CORS error should be fixed.

To identify the tense of a given sentence in MATLAB, you can use natural language processing (NLP) techniques and specifically, part-of-speech (POS) tagging. POS tagging assigns grammatical tags to words in a sentence, such as noun, verb, adjective, etc.

Here's a simple MATLAB code using the Natural Language Processing Toolbox to perform POS tagging on a sentence and determine its tense:

```MATLAB
% Load the sample sentence
sentence = 'I would like to have a meeting today';

% Tokenize the sentence into individual words
tokens = tokenized(sentence);

% Perform POS tagging on each word
tags = posTagged(tokens);

% Extract the verbs from the sentence
verbs = find(strcmp(tags, 'VB'));

% Check if there are any verbs in the sentence
if length(verbs) > 0
    % Determine the tense of the first verb
    tense = getTense(verbs(1));

    % Display the result
    disp(['The tense of the sentence is ', tense]);
else
    % If no verb is found, assume the sentence is not in the past or future tense
    disp('The sentence does not contain any verbs.');
end

function tense = getTense(verb)
    % This function identifies the tense of a verb based on its POS tag
    if strcmp(posTagged(verb), 'VBD')
        tense = 'Past';
    elseif strcmp(posTagged(verb), 'VBG')
        tense = 'Present';
    elseif strcmp(posTagged(verb), 'VBN')
        tense = 'Past participle';
    elseif strcmp(posTagged(verb), 'VBP')
        tense = 'Non-3rd person singular present';
    elseif strcmp(posTagged(verb), 'VBZ')
        tense = '3rd person singular present';
    else
        tense = 'Unknown';
    end
end
```

