Sure! Here's a PHP custom function "smart_sort_array" that accepts arguments "set" and "order":
```php
function smart_sort_array($set, $order) {
    // Sort elements of set by the key "age" of the sub-array, ordering it by the parameter "order" of the custom function "smart_sort_array"
    usort($set, function ($a, $b) use ($order) {
        if ($a['age'] === $b['age']) {
            return 0;
        }
        return ($order === 'asc') ? ($a['age'] < $b['age']) : ($a['age'] > $b['age']);
    });
    return $set;
}
```
To use this function, simply call it with your set and order parameters:
```php
$set = [
    ['name' => 'John', 'address' => '123 Main St', 'age' => 25],
    ['name' => 'Jane', 'address' => '456 Elm St', 'age' => 30],
    ['name' => 'Bob', 'address' => '789 Oak St', 'age' => 20]
];

$ordered_set = smart_sort_array($set, 'asc');
print_r($ordered_set);

$ordered_set = smart_sort_array($set, 'desc');
print_r($ordered_set);
```

Here's a JavaScript function that takes a string variable in dot notation and returns all entries in an object where the nested key value matches the string key:

```javascript
function getEntriesByKey(object, key) {
  const entries = [];

  Object.entries(object).forEach(([key2, value]) => {
    if (key2 === key) {
      entries.push({ key: key2, value });
    } else if (typeof value === 'object') {
      entries.push(...getEntriesByKey(value, key));
    }
  });

  return entries;
}
```

You can use this function like this:

```javascript
const object = {
  a: { b: 1 },
  c: { d: 2 },
  e: { f: 3 }
};

const key = 'b';

const entries = getEntriesByKey(object, key);
console.log(entries); // Output: [{ key: 'a.b', value: 1 }]
```

