To prevent your generated planets from being too close together, you can introduce a minimum distance between them during the generation process. You can achieve this by modifying your `generateRandomPlanets` function like so:

```javascript
const generateRandomPlanets = (numPlanets, minDistance) => {
  const planets = [];
  let attempts = 0;

  while (planets.length < numPlanets && attempts < 100) {
    attempts++;

    const planet = {
      position: [
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
      ],
      size: randomSize(1, 5) * scale,
      color: randomColor(),
      ringSize: 0.2 * scale,
      ringColor: randomColor(),
      ringNoise: 0.3 * scale,
    };

    // Check if planet is too close to any existing planet
    const tooClose = planets.some((p) =>
      p.position.some((coord, index) => {
        return Math.abs(coord - planet.position[index]) < minDistance
      })
    );

    if (!tooClose) {
      planets.push(planet);
    }
  }

  return planets;
};
```

In this modified version, we added a `minDistance` parameter to the function, which specifies the minimum distance between two planets. Inside the loop, we check if the newly generated planet is too close to any existing planet using the `tooClose` variable. If it's not too close, we add it to the `planets` array.

To use this modified function, simply call it with the desired number of planets and minimum distance, like this:

```javascript
const planets = generateRandomPlanets(5, 35);
```

This will ensure that the generated planets are spaced apart at least `minDistance` units away from each other. Adjust the `minDistance` value to control the density of your planets.

Here's a simple React utility that removes widows from text. It uses the `react-text` library to detect and wrap words that would create widows.

```jsx
import React, { useRef } from 'react';
import { Text } from 'react-text';

const WidowRemover = ({ children }) => {
  const textRef = useRef(null);

  const handleResize = () => {
    const textNode = textRef.current;
    if (textNode) {
      const width = textNode.offsetWidth;
      const lines = textNode.textContent.split('\n');

      let currentLine = 0;
      let currentWord = '';
      let previousWord = '';

      for (let i = 0; i < lines.length; i++) {
        const line = lines[i];
        const words = line.split(' ');

        for (let j = 0; j < words.length; j++) {
          const word = words[j];
          const wordWidth = textNode.getBoundingClientRect().width - textNode.left;

          if (wordWidth < 50) {
            currentWord = word;
            break;
          }

          previousWord = word;
        }

        if (currentWord !== previousWord) {
          lines[currentLine] += ` ${previousWord}`;
          currentLine++;
        }

        currentWord = '';
        previousWord = '';
      }

      textNode.textContent = lines.join('\n');
    }
  };

  return (
    <Text ref={textRef} onResize={handleResize}>
      {children}
    </Text>
  );
};

export default WidowRemover;
```

To use this component, you can simply wrap your text with it:

```jsx
<WidowRemover>
  Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet, consectetur adipiscing elit. Aliquam a turpis at purus malesuada feugiat.
</WidowRemover>
```

This component will automatically remove widows from the text by wrapping words

